# Nullish checks

Use loose equality **only** when testing both `null` and `undefined` together. This is an intentional exception to strict equality elsewhere.

| Intent | Prefer | Avoid |
|--------|--------|-------|
| Value is present (not nullish) | `value != null` | `value !== undefined && value !== null` |
| Value is absent (nullish) | `value == null` | `value === null \|\| value === undefined` |
| Default when nullish | `value ?? fallback` | (unchanged — keep using nullish coalescing) |

## When to use

- Filtering or skipping optional values
- Early returns when a param is missing
- Guards before reading a property that may be unset

## When not to use

- Do **not** use loose equality for any other comparison (strings, numbers, booleans, objects).
- When **`null` and `undefined` must be handled differently**, keep explicit checks.

## Examples

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
.filter(([, value]) => value != null && value !== false)

if (value == null) {
  return defaultValue;
}

// ❌ BAD — verbose equivalent
.filter(([, value]) => value !== undefined && value !== null && value !== false)

if (value === null || value === undefined) {
  return defaultValue;
}
```
